# Chris Wickham — Primary Method Extracts

Verified from primary sources, 2026-06-07.

## Extended Case Method (British Academy Interview, 2014)

> "You follow, as closely as possible, the lives of a set of individuals through time, using whatever sources you have, in order to see what their lives tell you about the society they lived in."

> "If between 85 and 95 per cent of the population of medieval Europe are peasants, it doesn't seem reasonable to me to restrict yourself to the study of the other tiny percentage."

> "History is the least theoretical of all disciplines... Historians have their own interpretative paradigms, and historians are always applying them, even if they don't state them."

## History from Below

> "I have an enormous fondness for the medieval peasantry, but I don't know what it's like to be them. I know what it's like to be me, and I think I know what it's like to be some of the elites that I study, and the peasants are a different kettle of fish."

> "If you are studying a peasant community from the outside, you are always looking at it through elite eyes. But you are studying the mass of the population."

## Comparative Method (Framing the Early Middle Ages, Introduction)

> "My overall research procedure, as I went from region to region, was to treat each in the same way, and to ask the same questions of each. This was in order to be able to make comparisons between regions which were as well founded as possible."

> "I do not think that a comparative framework in itself imposes a theoretical framework... the comparative method has the effect of making the analyst step back from the detail which envelops the single case-study and see wider patterns."

> "Comparison does not necessarily produce convergence: the point of comparison is to see both similarities and differences clearly."

## Weberian Ideal Types (Framing, p.36)

Wickham explicitly uses Weberian ideal types for cross-regional comparison. This is NOT Marxist materialism — it's comparative sociology using materialist tools.

## Surplus Extraction (Magistra et Mater Blog)

> "A socio-economic system based on the exploitative relations of production between peasants and lords" — on the feudal mode.

> Peasants could withdraw from the market and sustain themselves, which is why the feudal economy persisted — lords couldn't force market integration.

## Key Method Insight

Wickham's method asks: **what is the actual mechanism by which surplus moves from producer to extractor?** Not ideology, not culture, not personality — the infrastructure of extraction. Tax policy, bureaucracy, land tenure, labor obligations.

## Applied to Vietnam

- French colonial tax extraction (rubber, rice, opium)
- Nguyen mandarin examination/bureaucracy system
- Viet Minh tax resistance as political strategy
- Diem's strategic hamlets as extraction infrastructure
- CIA covert funding as alternative extraction system
